  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/c2644fc906d4588bc39aa92d887836ff8942f5eb4b28de1baf3dc1030d2dca67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{3},$ what is the value of $cos\u2061\\theta ?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13932638/assets/4942cec8983dd2bddddd0ba2f8f9332b1b82f3999a44adb138b905457d39c633.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos\u2061\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$",
